---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/product-launch';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/product-launch');
const desc = 'Plan product-launch SMS around verified availability, permission, allocation, service capacity, purchase exits, and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Product launch campaigns</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">SMS marketing apps for product launch campaigns</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A launch message should open a real buying opportunity for an eligible recipient. Tie every invitation to a release version, a purchasable variant, the right market, and a queue that can stop when the promise changes.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app profiles</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">Campaign restraint</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Give the release an owner and a version</h2>
      <p>Maintain a release record with product and variant IDs, market, sales channel, approved copy, price and currency, access opening, offer expiry, and the person allowed to pause it. Version that record when timing, eligibility, stock allocation, or the offer changes. A queued invitation should reference the approved version rather than a mutable campaign name.</p>
      <p>Use commerce data as the authority for purchasability and inventory; use the approved release record for embargo and allocation policy. Shopify describes product publishing across channels and markets in its <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/products/product-status">product status and availability documentation</a>. A scheduled message is not evidence that a product can be bought. Check the actual destination, selected variant, market price, shipping eligibility, and checkout before releasing each wave.</p>
      <p>Separate ready-to-ship launches from presales. For a presale, approve the expected fulfillment language, payment terms, cancellation route, and capacity with the responsible teams. If those details are unresolved, keep the invitation paused rather than promising immediate dispatch. An aggregate stock count cannot justify a scarcity claim about a particular size or market.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>A waitlist is an interest record, not blanket permission</h2>
      <p>Record what the person requested: one variant alert, a collection launch, presale access, or ongoing marketing. Keep the capture wording, source, time, channel permission, and withdrawal state. Do not turn a product-interest form or an order phone number into permission for unrelated promotional texts. Have the relevant team review destination-specific requirements; these operating controls do not establish legal compliance.</p>
      <p>Match the waitlist entry to a stable customer identity and a verified contact destination. Resolve duplicate entries without extending permission. Shared phone numbers, changed addresses, and ambiguous profile merges need review; do not infer that two people share an interest or consent. VIP status can determine access policy, but does not replace permission.</p>
      <p>Tell subscribers whether joining creates a reservation, a place in line, or only an alert. An invitation link should not imply reserved stock unless an enforceable reservation exists. Keep waitlist withdrawal distinct from a marketing opt-out while honoring each applicable scope. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent operations</a> for capture planning.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Embargo, local sending windows, and offer expiry</h2>
      <p>Store the release opening and expiry as explicit instants with the intended timezone. Treat the product embargo separately from the recipient's permitted sending window. Recheck both at dispatch, including daylight-saving changes and uncertain timezone data. If a delayed invitation would arrive after access closes or with too little time to act under your approved policy, retire it. Do not send an expired countdown merely because it passed an earlier audience check.</p>
      <p>Review preview links, public product pages, email, SMS, and other channels for premature access. A hidden link is not an access control. Decide how forwarded links and early visitors will be handled at checkout. Ensure the destination states the same price, eligibility, deadline, and reservation terms as the invitation. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours guide</a> when designing local scheduling controls; there is no universal launch-hour recommendation here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Release invitations in capacity-aware waves</h2>
      <p>Set allocation by variant and market after accounting for existing commitments, presales, safety stock, and fulfillment limits. Document the priority rule before selecting recipients: for example, request order within each eligible variant group. Do not quietly substitute lifetime spend for an advertised first-come policy. If you use a lottery or VIP window, explain that policy before capture.</p>
      <p>Start a wave only when the store, payment path, inventory feed, support desk, and warehouse can handle it. Choose the next wave from current capacity and demand, rather than a fixed percentage of sell-through. Observe checkout errors, queue age, unfulfilled commitments, replies, and variant availability. Pause when the named owner cannot confirm the promise. Waitlist size and historical click rates do not guarantee sell-through or a safe audience size.</p>
      <p>For a hypothetical allocation worksheet, 100 units minus 20 existing commitments minus 10 safety units leaves 70 units available for the release. That is a stock ceiling, not permission to invite exactly 70 people: quantity limits, reservation enforcement, and concurrent channels still matter. Reconcile actual orders before authorizing another wave.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Service takes priority across journeys</h2>
      <p>Assign one contact-level decision point across launch, welcome, cart, back-in-stock, loyalty, and winback programs. A delivery problem, payment issue, cancellation request, or active support conversation should pause conflicting launch promotion under an explicit policy. Keep necessary service messages on their own permission and routing rules; a promotional pause must not silently discard an order update.</p>
      <p>At dispatch, recheck purchase, waitlist withdrawal, channel opt-out, identity, holdout assignment, capacity, and the combined contact budget. A confirmed purchase of the launched variant should retire that variant's invitation and reminders. Define collection-level exits deliberately so a purchase does not trigger an unwanted upsell to every remaining item. A refund or cancellation must not automatically re-enroll the buyer into a launch that has already closed.</p>
      <p>Coordinate email education and text access around the same release and suppression state. Email can carry specifications and presale detail; a short text may carry a timely invitation when permitted and useful. Neither channel earns a universal cadence. Read <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">email and SMS coordination</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">post-purchase operations</a> when defining the handoff.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Late events, retries, and closing the queue</h2>
      <p>Deduplicate by release version, recipient, variant, and invitation purpose. Track event occurrence time separately from receipt time; a late interest event must not undo a newer withdrawal or purchase. Reconcile against authoritative current state when events conflict. If purchase or permission feeds are stale beyond the approved tolerance, pause promotion rather than assuming eligibility.</p>
      <p>A delivery timeout is ambiguous. Reconcile sender acknowledgement before retrying so a delayed response does not create a second invitation. Bound retries by release expiry, current permission, and capacity; a provider accepting a message does not prove delivery or purchase. Retire pending work when a release closes, sells out, is withdrawn, or changes version. Preserve suppression and dispatch history so importing a fresh list does not resurrect retired work.</p>
      <p>For recovery, name an incident owner, stop affected queues across senders, reconcile purchases and reservations, inspect a small sample, and approve a new version only if a valid opportunity remains. Do not replay the outage backlog indiscriminately. Decide whether an accurate correction is needed for people already contacted, using the appropriate channel and permission. Record why each queued item was sent, paused, or retired.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical scenarios and proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are proposed acceptance fixtures for an operator's implementation, not reported merchant outcomes or claims that any named app passes them.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wrong market:</strong> a product is visible in one market but the recipient's variant cannot ship to theirs. Expect no invitation and a recorded availability reason.</li>
        <li><strong>Waitlist versus presale:</strong> someone requested a single restock alert and has no matching launch permission. Expect exclusion from the presale broadcast; joining the waitlist must not create a paid reservation.</li>
        <li><strong>Embargo and delay:</strong> a local sending window starts after a short access offer expires. Expect the invitation to retire, with no obsolete countdown.</li>
        <li><strong>Allocation exhausted:</strong> another channel consumes the remaining size allocation between selection and dispatch. Expect that size's wave to pause while other variants are evaluated independently.</li>
        <li><strong>Service collision:</strong> a subscriber enters the launch queue while support investigates an unfulfilled order. Expect promotion to pause under the shared policy while necessary service communication continues.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase and withdrawal:</strong> a purchase or withdrawal arrives before a scheduled reminder; an older interest event arrives later. Expect the reminder to remain retired, including after an import or profile reconciliation.</li>
        <li><strong>Ambiguous retry:</strong> the sender accepts an invitation but the acknowledgement times out. Expect reconciliation before any retry and no duplicate dispatch for that invitation key.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery after closure:</strong> an outage ends after the release sold out. Expect the backlog to remain retired; reopening requires current availability, permission checks, and explicit release approval.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Choose a stack around the release controls</h2>
      <p>A dedicated sender can fit when the team already has a shared source for permission and launch state. A combined channel system can fit when one team owns both channels and can demonstrate common exits. A separate education layer can fit when it receives reliable purchase and withdrawal updates. These are architecture choices, not supplier capability claims.</p>
      <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> to shortlist options. Ask each candidate to demonstrate the fixtures above with your market, variants, identity model, and event delays. Confirm current contract terms, fees, queue cancellation behavior, exportability, and who owns incident response. Compare total release costs, including integration work and support, rather than assuming a revenue band determines the right supplier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution without confusing allocation with lift</h2>
      <p>Assign eligible contacts to treatment and holdout before dispatch and retain assignment across channels. Define whether the experiment measures SMS added to the same email program or the whole coordinated launch. Keep service messages available to both groups. Predefine the observation window, purchase exits, costs, and refund treatment; allow time for returns before treating the result as settled.</p>
      <p>Scarce stock complicates holdouts: one person's purchase can remove another person's opportunity. Record stock exposure, access windows, and wave membership; avoid interpreting faster sell-through as incremental total demand. Where fair allocation permits, use comparable release cohorts or repeated launches to investigate the effect, and report uncertainty and spillover rather than claiming a causal win from attributed sales.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical arithmetic: 1,000 eligible treatment contacts produce $3,000 of contribution after product costs, discounts, fulfillment, and expected returns; 1,000 holdout contacts produce $2,700 on the same basis. The difference is $0.30 per eligible contact, or $300 across the treatment group. Subtract $120 of incremental messaging/platform expense and $50 of additional support to leave $130. Count each cost once. This illustration is not a forecast, and small samples or unequal stock access can make that difference uninformative.</p>
      <p>Report contribution per eligible contact alongside total contribution, fulfilled orders, opt-outs, complaints, support demand, and queue retirement reasons. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> before expanding the next release.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
